.TH QSORT 3 "November 12, 2026"
.UC 4
.SH NAME
qsort, qsort_r \- sort an array of records
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <stdlib.h>
.PP
.B void qsort(void *base, size_t nel, size_t width,
.B "    int (*compar)(const void *, const void *));"
.PP
.B void qsort_r(void *base, size_t nel, size_t width,
.B "    int (*compar)(const void *, const void *, void *), void *arg);"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Qsort
sorts an array in place.
The first argument is a pointer to the base of the data;
the second is the number of elements;
the third is the width of an element in bytes;
the last is the name of the comparison routine
to be called with two arguments which are pointers
to the elements being compared.
The routine must return an integer less than, equal to, or greater than 0
according as the first argument is to be considered
less than, equal to, or greater than the second.
.PP
.I Qsort_r
passes
.I arg
unchanged as the third argument to each comparison.
Its declaration is available by default and when
.B _POSIX_C_SOURCE
is at least 202405L or
.B _XOPEN_SOURCE
is at least 800.
.PP
The comparison routine defines a consistent ordering and leaves record
contents unchanged.
The sort preserves every record's bytes and multiplicity.
The relative order of records that compare equal is unspecified.
An array with zero elements causes neither comparisons nor rearrangement.
Comparison routines can sort separate arrays recursively; each call retains
its own comparator, element width and context.
.SH "RETURN VALUE"
These functions return no value.
.SH STANDARDS
.I Qsort
implements the ISO C17 sorting interface.
.I Qsort_r
uses the POSIX.1-2024 context-last signature.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
sort(1)
